From 32c89ed7d94de8d985d3bd7c9281a8d4a29521b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Currie <rob@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 13:52:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat: a whole TRef member of a split collection, written
 as ROOT writes one

`roottest`'s io/references/lotsRef splits a TClonesArray of a macro's A, and
A holds a TRef of its own: round 13 gave the writer ROOT's description of
TRef, and the entry is now written by it. A member held by value says how
long it is through its class, so its ten bytes in the entry are TRef's
version and then TObject's identifier and bits - no byte count in front,
which only a record of its own carries. That is what the oracle writes for
the same macro (C1._o is 00010000000100000000): the same basket.
SplitArray.written no longer refuses the member, and wbranch's column for
such a member is given the TRefs themselves, one row per entry, and streams
each by the member's class.

lotsRef300 and lotsRef2 pass; lotsRef1 and lotsRef are still REFUSED-turned-
FAILED by two pyroot blockers outside this chain, reported rather than
touched: TFile::Write writes back a tree it read (TTree::Write refuses a tree
read from a file), and the same for Close's _write_trees.

tests: held_object's bytes against the oracle's, its refusal by name, the
column that streams a row of TRefs, and the attribute/member readers.
